---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../site.config.ts";
import { policy } from "../lib/policy.ts";
import { cacheFor } from "../lib/http.ts";
import { UA } from "../lib/feeds/get.ts";

const decision = policy({ type: "static" });
Astro.response.headers.set("Cache-Control", cacheFor(3600));
---

<Base
  title="About rss.mobi"
  description="How the rss.mobi directory of RSS feeds works: who can add a feed, how often feeds are checked, what is shown, and how to take a feed down."
  robots={decision.robots}
  path="/about/"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About this RSS feeds directory</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      rss.mobi is a public list of RSS feeds, built to be read on a phone. Anyone can add a feed, and anyone can find one and follow it in the
      reader they already use.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a feed</h2>
    <p>
      Paste a site or feed address on the <a href="/submit/" title="Submit an RSS feed to the directory">submit page</a>. RSS 2.0, RSS 1.0,
      Atom and JSON Feed are all read. There is no account and no review queue: a feed that can be read, has at least one post and is not spam
      is published at once. Whoever submits it gets a private edit link to change its topics or take it down.
    </p>

    <h2>What is shown</h2>
    <p>
      The feed's title, description and the latest posts it publishes: each post's title, a short excerpt, its date and a link to the original.
      Posts older than ninety days drop out.
    </p>

    <h2>A copy of every feed</h2>
    <p>
      Every feed's page offers an RSS address of its own at rss.mobi to follow in any reader. It carries what the original carries — whole posts,
      pictures, a podcast's episodes — read from the original when a reader asks, never more than five minutes old, and never stored. The one who
      submitted a feed can keep its copy to excerpts from their edit link.
    </p>

    <h2>How feeds are checked</h2>
    <p>
      Every feed is fetched about once an hour by a crawler that identifies itself as <code>{UA}</code>. It sends the ETag and Last-Modified the
      site gave last time, so an unchanged feed costs a 304 and nothing else. A feed that fails is tried less and less often, and after ten
      failures in a row it is retired.
    </p>
    <p>
      Readers such as Feedly and Inoreader say in their user agent how many people follow a feed. When they fetch a copy from rss.mobi, that
      number is passed on to the original the same way (<code>; 16 subscribers</code> at the end of ours), so a publisher's count of followers
      stays whole.
    </p>

    <h2>Taking a feed down</h2>
    <p>
      If you run a site and do not want its feed here, open the feed's page and use <strong>Report a problem → I own this site</strong>, or write to
      <a href={`mailto:${site.contact}`} title="Complaints and removal requests for the RSS feeds directory">{site.contact}</a>. You can also
      block the user agent above; a feed that keeps refusing is retired on its own.
    </p>

    <h2>For developers</h2>
    <p>
      The directory is available as JSON at <a href="/api/v1/feeds" title="The RSS feeds directory as JSON"><code>/api/v1/feeds</code></a>, and
      each feed at <code>/api/v1/feeds/&lt;slug&gt;</code>. No key is needed to read.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code that runs this RSS feeds directory is open source under the AGPL-3.0, at
      <a href="https://github.com/dstengine/rss-mobi" title="Source code of this RSS feeds directory, AGPL-3.0">github.com/dstengine/rss-mobi</a>.
      Issues and pull requests are welcome.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
